    <div class="row g-4">
        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Friendly Introduction</h2>
                <p>When you start learning system design, these three terms come up very quickly: <strong>monolithic</strong>, <strong>layered</strong>, and <strong>distributed</strong>. Many learners get confused because they sound like alternatives at the same level, but in real projects they often represent an evolution path.</p>
                <p class="mb-0">In this lesson, we will use an e-commerce example to see how each architecture works, why teams choose it, and how to transition without creating unnecessary risk.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">In this lesson, we will discuss</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li>The core idea behind monolithic, layered, and distributed systems.</li>
                    <li>A practical step-by-step journey from startup stage to scale stage.</li>
                    <li>Benefits and limitations of each approach.</li>
                    <li>Best practices and common migration mistakes.</li>
                    <li>MCQs and interview-ready Q&A for revision.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12 col-xl-6">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4 h-100">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Architecture Diagram: Side-by-Side View</h2>
                <div class="diagram-card mb-2">
                    <img src="~/css/Images/Microservices/monolithic-layered-distributed-comparison.svg" alt="Monolithic vs Layered vs Distributed system architecture comparison diagram" loading="lazy" />
                </div>
                <p class="mb-0"><small class="text-muted">This visual gives you the fastest way to remember how deployment boundaries and data boundaries change between styles.</small></p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12 col-xl-6">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4 h-100">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">E-commerce Evolution Diagram</h2>
                <div class="diagram-card mb-2">
                    <img src="~/css/Images/Microservices/ecommerce-architecture-evolution.svg" alt="E-commerce architecture evolution from monolith to layered monolith to distributed services" loading="lazy" />
                </div>
                <p class="mb-0"><small class="text-muted">A realistic migration path: improve structure first, then extract only high-pressure modules as services.</small></p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Step-by-Step Explanation</h2>

                <h3 class="h5 mt-2">Step 1: Monolithic architecture (single deployable)</h3>
                <p>A monolith is one application deployed as one unit. Catalog, cart, order, payment, and admin modules all live in one codebase and usually share one database. This is great for speed in early stages.</p>
                <pre class="diagram-box mb-3">[E-commerce Web App]
 ├─ Catalog
 ├─ Cart
 ├─ Orders
 ├─ Payments
 └─ Admin
      ↓
   Single DB</pre>

                <h3 class="h5">Step 2: Layered architecture (better organization inside one app)</h3>
                <p>Layered architecture keeps one deployable app, but separates responsibilities into layers (UI/API, business logic, data access). You still deploy one application, but the code is cleaner and easier to maintain.</p>
                <pre class="diagram-box mb-3">Presentation Layer (MVC/API)
         ↓
Business Layer (Domain Rules)
         ↓
Data Layer (Repositories/ORM)
         ↓
       Database</pre>

                <h3 class="h5">Step 3: Distributed system (many deployables)</h3>
                <p>Now business capabilities become independent services. Each service can be deployed and scaled separately. This is powerful at scale but introduces networking, observability, and consistency challenges.</p>
                <pre class="diagram-box mb-0">Client → API Gateway
  ├→ Catalog Service → Catalog DB
  ├→ Order Service   → Order DB
  ├→ Payment Service → Payment DB
  └→ Notification Service
  (API + Event communication)</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Mini Case Study: E-commerce Festival Sale</h2>
                <p><strong>Initial stage:</strong> One team launches quickly with a monolithic app. Daily traffic is manageable, and one release pipeline is enough.</p>
                <p><strong>Growth stage:</strong> New features like coupons, returns, and seller onboarding make controllers heavy and business rules scattered. Team adopts layered architecture to keep code maintainable.</p>
                <p><strong>Scale stage:</strong> During festival sales, payment and order traffic spikes 10x. Team extracts Order and Payment as independent services while keeping some modules in the monolith. This selective extraction improves release speed and scaling.</p>
                <div class="info-box mb-0">
                    Industry tip: the best migration is usually <strong>incremental</strong> (modular monolith first, then targeted service extraction), not a one-shot rewrite.
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Benefits / Advantages</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Monolithic:</strong> low operational overhead and fast MVP delivery.</li>
                    <li><strong>Layered:</strong> better maintainability, testability, and clearer responsibilities.</li>
                    <li><strong>Distributed:</strong> independent deployment, targeted scaling, and better team autonomy.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Challenges / Limitations</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Monolithic:</strong> slow releases as codebase and team size grow.</li>
                    <li><strong>Layered:</strong> cleaner code, but still one release unit and one blast radius.</li>
                    <li><strong>Distributed:</strong> service communication failures, eventual consistency, and higher DevOps demands.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="warning-box mt-3 mb-0">Avoid choosing distributed architecture only because it is trendy. Choose it when clear business pain points require it.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Comparison with Related Concepts</h2>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table class="table table-bordered align-middle comparison-table mb-0">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Aspect</th>
                                <th>Monolithic</th>
                                <th>Layered</th>
                                <th>Distributed</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Deployment model</td>
                                <td>Single deployable</td>
                                <td>Single deployable</td>
                                <td>Multiple deployables</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Internal structure</td>
                                <td>May become mixed over time</td>
                                <td>Strictly separated by layers</td>
                                <td>Separated by business capability</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Scaling approach</td>
                                <td>Scale full app</td>
                                <td>Scale full app</td>
                                <td>Scale hot services only</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Data ownership</td>
                                <td>Usually shared DB</td>
                                <td>Usually shared DB</td>
                                <td>Commonly database per service</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Operational complexity</td>
                                <td>Low</td>
                                <td>Low to medium</td>
                                <td>High</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Best fit</td>
                                <td>Startups, MVPs</td>
                                <td>Growing products with one deployable</td>
                                <td>High-scale, multi-team platforms</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Best Practices / Tips / Common Mistakes</h2>
                <div class="row g-3">
                    <div class="col-lg-6">
                        <div class="good-box h-100">
                            <h3 class="h6 fw-bold">Best Practices</h3>
                            <ul class="mb-0 list-tight">
                                <li>Build clear module boundaries before any migration.</li>
                                <li>Extract one high-value service first (for example, payments).</li>
                                <li>Use API contracts, versioning, and idempotent event handlers.</li>
                                <li>Set up centralized logging, metrics, tracing, and CI/CD early.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-lg-6">
                        <div class="bad-box h-100">
                            <h3 class="h6 fw-bold">Common Mistakes</h3>
                            <ul class="mb-0 list-tight">
                                <li>Splitting by technical layers (UI service, DAL service) instead of business domains.</li>
                                <li>Creating too many tiny services too soon.</li>
                                <li>Sharing one database across all services after migration.</li>
                                <li>Ignoring failure handling (timeouts, retries, circuit breakers).</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Summary</h2>
                <p class="mb-0">Monolithic architecture is excellent for fast initial delivery. Layered architecture is a cleaner way to grow that monolith safely. Distributed systems become valuable when business scale and team structure demand independent deployment and scaling. In practice, successful teams evolve gradually, not abruptly.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">MCQ Practice (with Answers)</h2>
                <ol class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Which architecture has a single deployable unit?</strong><br />
                        <strong>Answer:</strong> Monolithic architecture.</li>
                    <li><strong>In layered architecture, what is the main purpose of separating Presentation, Business, and Data layers?</strong><br />
                        <strong>Answer:</strong> To improve maintainability, readability, and testability inside one application.</li>
                    <li><strong>What is a key operational challenge in distributed systems?</strong><br />
                        <strong>Answer:</strong> Handling network failures, tracing requests, and maintaining observability across services.</li>
                    <li><strong>During a sales spike, only Payment service traffic increases. Which architecture benefits most from targeted scaling?</strong><br />
                        <strong>Answer:</strong> Distributed systems (microservices).</li>
                    <li><strong>True or False: Layered architecture and microservices are the same concept.</strong><br />
                        <strong>Answer:</strong> False. Layering is internal structuring; microservices are independent deployables.</li>
                    <li><strong>What is usually a safer migration path for a growing product?</strong><br />
                        <strong>Answer:</strong> Monolith → Modular/layered monolith → Selective service extraction.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Interview Questions and Answers</h2>
                <div class="accordion" id="interviewQaAccordion">
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="mq1"><button class="accordion-button" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ma1" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="ma1">1) Monolithic vs distributed: what is the most practical difference?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ma1" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" aria-labelledby="mq1" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">A monolith is built and released as one unit, while distributed systems are split into independent services that can be deployed and scaled separately.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="mq2"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ma2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ma2">2) If layered architecture is still one app, why do teams adopt it?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ma2" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="mq2" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Because it keeps concerns separated, makes business logic cleaner, improves testing, and reduces long-term code chaos even without moving to microservices.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="mq3"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ma3" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ma3">3) What signs indicate it is time to extract services from a monolith?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ma3" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="mq3" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Repeated release bottlenecks, parts of the app needing different scaling patterns, and multiple teams blocking each other in the same codebase.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="mq4"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ma4" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ma4">4) Why is "database per service" often recommended in distributed architecture?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ma4" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="mq4" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">It protects service autonomy. Shared databases create hidden coupling and make independent deployment and schema evolution difficult.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="mq5"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ma5" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ma5">5) Give one e-commerce module that is commonly extracted first and why.</button></h2>
                        <div id="ma5" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="mq5" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Payments or Orders are common first candidates because they usually have high traffic, independent scaling needs, and clear business boundaries.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="mq6"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ma6" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ma6">6) What new responsibilities come with distributed systems besides coding?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ma6" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="mq6" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Service discovery, monitoring, distributed tracing, resilient communication patterns, deployment orchestration, and incident response.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="mq7"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ma7" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ma7">7) What is a common migration mistake interviewers expect candidates to mention?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ma7" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="mq7" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Trying a big-bang rewrite instead of incremental extraction. It increases risk, delays value delivery, and often causes production instability.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
